WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.363
Welcome. This is Knowledge-Centric Agents for Workflow Generation in ComfyUI,

2
00:00:05.363 --> 00:00:10.702
our ECCV 2026 paper from INSAIT at Sofia University, together with Adobe Research.

3
00:00:10.702 --> 00:00:16.105
We study how to build an agent that turns a plain task description into a complete,

4
00:00:16.105 --> 00:00:17.798
runnable ComfyUI workflow.

5
00:00:18.698 --> 00:00:21.473
ComfyUI is a node-based system where an image

6
00:00:21.473 --> 00:00:24.495
generation pipeline is built as a directed graph.

7
00:00:24.495 --> 00:00:26.839
Every node is a model or an operation,

8
00:00:26.839 --> 00:00:29.861
and the entire workflow is stored as a JSON file.

9
00:00:29.861 --> 00:00:33.130
Building a good one by hand takes real expertise. So:

10
00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:34.733
can an agent do it for us?

11
00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:39.297
Given an instruction like, stylize this photo, enhance the facial details,

12
00:00:39.297 --> 00:00:44.478
and match the colours back to the original, our agent derives a high-level strategy,

13
00:00:44.478 --> 00:00:47.007
composes the corresponding ComfyUI graph,

14
00:00:47.007 --> 00:00:50.522
and then actually executes it to produce the final image.

15
00:00:51.422 --> 00:00:55.451
Most prior work treats this as direct text-to-JSON generation,

16
00:00:55.451 --> 00:00:58.310
and that struggles for two distinct reasons.

17
00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:03.249
At the strategic level, workflow design is open-ended and experience-driven.

18
00:01:03.249 --> 00:01:08.057
Whether to segment, how to split branches, which control modules interact:

19
00:01:08.057 --> 00:01:13.126
designers rely on tacit expertise that is simply not present in ordinary text.

20
00:01:13.126 --> 00:01:18.389
At the structural level, workflows are large discrete graphs and JSON is brittle.

21
00:01:18.389 --> 00:01:20.988
One misconnected link, and nothing runs.

22
00:01:20.988 --> 00:01:22.418
The evidence is stark.

23
00:01:22.418 --> 00:01:25.862
GPT-5 prompted zero-shot scores zero on every metric.

24
00:01:26.762 --> 00:01:31.253
Our claim is that workflow generation requires modelling knowledge itself:

25
00:01:31.253 --> 00:01:34.712
its structure, its hierarchy, and its reasoning dynamics.

26
00:01:34.712 --> 00:01:36.290
We do this in three steps.

27
00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:38.960
We invert knowledge out of large collections

28
00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:41.691
of real workflows into an explicit hierarchy.

29
00:01:41.691 --> 00:01:46.243
We inject the parts that genuinely require reasoning into a language model.

30
00:01:46.243 --> 00:01:50.066
And at test time we infer, by running that hierarchy backwards.

31
00:01:50.966 --> 00:01:55.630
Inversion proceeds bottom-up over nine hundred and twelve curated real workflows.

32
00:01:55.630 --> 00:02:00.237
A purely rule-based step first converts each raw workflow into full pseudo-code,

33
00:02:00.237 --> 00:02:02.598
keeping every module, link and parameter.

34
00:02:02.598 --> 00:02:07.089
A language model then abstracts away the values, leaving skeleton pseudo-code:

35
00:02:07.089 --> 00:02:08.759
just the functional topology.

36
00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:10.775
Next we recover strategy reasoning,

37
00:02:10.775 --> 00:02:15.497
traces explaining why the graph is organised this way, why two controls are fused,

38
00:02:15.497 --> 00:02:17.858
why face detailing comes after denoising.

39
00:02:17.858 --> 00:02:21.025
That is summarised into a concise strategy description,

40
00:02:21.025 --> 00:02:24.998
and finally into the task instruction that a user would have written.

41
00:02:25.898 --> 00:02:27.910
Not every level needs learning.

42
00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:31.089
We fine-tune exactly where reasoning is required:

43
00:02:31.089 --> 00:02:36.150
task to strategy, which carries the tacit expertise, and strategy to skeleton,

44
00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:39.070
which needs compositional graph construction.

45
00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:43.158
Restoring parameters is pattern-driven, so it stays rule-based.

46
00:02:43.158 --> 00:02:48.155
An auxiliary objective makes the model spell out why each decision was taken.

47
00:02:48.155 --> 00:02:52.178
The backbone is Qwen3-14B with LoRA, trained on a single H200.

48
00:02:53.078 --> 00:02:54.990
Inference is the mirror image.

49
00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:59.770
A high-level strategy planner turns the instruction into a processing plan.

50
00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:03.339
A pseudo-code generator turns that plan into a skeleton.

51
00:03:03.339 --> 00:03:07.482
A pretrained model fills in parameters and bindings from context.

52
00:03:07.482 --> 00:03:11.880
And a rule-based reconstruction step converts the result into a real,

53
00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:13.473
executable ComfyUI graph.

54
00:03:13.473 --> 00:03:18.062
After each learned stage, the model re-reads and refines its own output.

55
00:03:18.962 --> 00:03:21.852
On our thirty-task test set the gains are large.

56
00:03:21.852 --> 00:03:25.163
Valid node diversity reaches one hundred and fifty-two,

57
00:03:25.163 --> 00:03:28.234
more than three and a half times the best baseline,

58
00:03:28.234 --> 00:03:30.522
so the workflows are genuinely richer.

59
00:03:30.522 --> 00:03:34.736
Node and link completeness exceed ninety-six and ninety-eight percent.

60
00:03:34.736 --> 00:03:36.663
And most importantly, execution:

61
00:03:36.663 --> 00:03:40.757
every workflow was run manually in ComfyUI and inspected by a human.

62
00:03:40.757 --> 00:03:45.152
Our pass rate is eighty-seven percent, against thirty-six for ComfyAgent,

63
00:03:45.152 --> 00:03:46.898
and the resolve rate doubles.

64
00:03:47.798 --> 00:03:49.280
These gains carry over.

65
00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.853
On ComfyBench and on FlowBench we lead on both structure and execution.

66
00:03:53.853 --> 00:03:56.816
And one control experiment worth highlighting:

67
00:03:56.816 --> 00:04:01.454
training Qwen3-32B end-to-end, straight from instruction to pseudo-code,

68
00:04:01.454 --> 00:04:03.902
reaches only fifty-seven percent pass,

69
00:04:03.902 --> 00:04:07.381
while our fourteen-billion model reaches eighty-seven.

70
00:04:07.381 --> 00:04:08.798
Hierarchy beats scale.

71
00:04:09.698 --> 00:04:13.227
Qualitatively, the baselines often fail to execute at all,

72
00:04:13.227 --> 00:04:15.721
or silently drop part of the instruction.

73
00:04:15.721 --> 00:04:19.129
Here, few-shot prompting produces a muddled composition.

74
00:04:19.129 --> 00:04:22.232
Retrieval-augmented generation renders the airship,

75
00:04:22.232 --> 00:04:26.856
but stops at five hundred and twelve pixels, ignoring the upscaling request.

76
00:04:26.856 --> 00:04:29.472
ComfyAgent never produces a runnable graph.

77
00:04:29.472 --> 00:04:32.027
Our agent follows the full instruction and

78
00:04:32.027 --> 00:04:34.826
delivers the requested high-resolution result.

79
00:04:35.726 --> 00:04:39.932
To summarise: model the knowledge, not the raw graph. Explicit,

80
00:04:39.932 --> 00:04:45.272
reusable knowledge levels beat both handcrafted schemas and end-to-end training.

81
00:04:45.272 --> 00:04:50.078
One limitation is that rare node types get substituted with common ones.

82
00:04:50.078 --> 00:04:53.750
Thank you, and please come and find our poster at ECCV.
